<article class="env-definition" id="048V">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/048V">Definition <span data-tag="048V">83.5.8</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme, and let $B$ be an algebraic space over $S$. Let $j : R \to U \times _ B U$ be a pre-relation over $B$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say $\phi : U \to X$ is <em>set-theoretically $R$-invariant</em> if and only if the map $U(k) \to X(k)$ equalizes the two maps $s, t : R(k) \to U(k)$ for every algebraically closed field $k$ over $B$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say $\phi : U \to X$ <em>separates orbits</em>, or <em>separates $R$-orbits</em> if it is set-theoretically $R$-invariant and $\phi (\overline{u}) = \phi (\overline{u}')$ in $X(k)$ implies that $\overline{u}, \overline{u}' \in U(k)$ are in the same orbit for every algebraically closed field $k$ over $B$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>